/-
  * Examples.RwxFlagNs.TargetMemImage — the TARGET binary's ELF snapshot

  SNAPSHOT OUTPUT — the bytes below are what `elf-snapshot` produced for
  this binary, do not hand-edit them.  The hand-written parts are the note
  below and the sanity section at the end.

  The image is the ordinary lld layout — THREE LOAD segments, no page both
  writable and executable:

  - `0x1000000`, `r--`, 4 KiB — ELF headers and the program header table
    (`0x1000040`, six entries);
  - `0x1001000`, `r-x`, 4 KiB — `.text`: six instructions, entry
    `0x1001190`;
  - `0x1002000`, `rw-`, 4 KiB — `.data`: the writable `flag` byte at
    `0x10021a8`.

  So this side of the pair is the standard static-fetch machinery — the
  `x /\ ~w` gate of `RiscvSemantics.code_read` accepts `.text`, and the
  overlay is never consulted for a fetch.

  HOW THE BYTES GET HERE.  Same as `SourceMemImage.lean` (read that note
  for the why): each segment's 4096 bytes are written out as lowercase hex
  in 8-byte chunks, four chunks (32 bytes) to a line with the line's byte
  offset in a comment.

  ** A note on the committed payloads.**  All three `.hex` files hold the
  same 4096 bytes; they are what `elf-snapshot` produced for this image,
  and this file reproduces them byte for byte rather than re-deriving
  what each page "should" contain.  It is
  harmless here, and the reason is worth stating: every `PT_LOAD` file
  offset (`0x0`, `0x190`, `0x1a8`) is below a page, and each segment's
  page starts at its own `p_vaddr` page boundary, so page offset `f` of
  every segment is file offset `f` too.  The payloads therefore place
  every byte the program touches at the address the program headers say
  it lives at — `.text` at page offset `0x190` of the `r-x` segment
  (`0x1001190`), the `flag` byte at page offset `0x1a8` of the `rw-`
  segment (`0x10021a8`).  The bytes beyond each segment's own `p_filesz`
  extent are page fill the program never reads; they are kept because
  that is the snapshot that was checked in.
-/
import LinuxSem.LinuxProcessSem.MemImageType

/- The byte-level checks at the end go through the model's own hex
   decoder (`segment_read_bytes`, `segment_byte_at`). -/
import LinuxSem.LinuxProcessSem.RiscvSemantics

/- `autoImplicit` is off: a bare field name in a statement would
   otherwise become an implicitly bound variable, i.e. a weakened claim
   rather than a compile error. -/
set_option autoImplicit false

/- As in `SourceMemImage.lean`. -/
set_option maxRecDepth 40000

